{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-252989",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a set $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the infimum of $A$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)+2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)+2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)+2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)+2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(A) & =min\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
